+Datacenter physical security
+• Data centers hosting frontier model workloads meet our physical security standard before sensitive hardware or workloads arrive, and operate under binding, auditable security requirements for as long as they host them.
+• Hardware moves in and out of these facilities only under verified authorization and an auditable chain of custody, media leaving our custody yields no recoverable data, and physical access to compute areas is limited to individually authenticated, vetted personnel with a documented need.
+• These facilities are monitored continuously and centrally with prompt response to physical intrusion, and public exposure of sensitive information about them and the people who work in them is detected and handled as a security incident.
+Platform Security
+• In sensitive environments, each host treats attached devices as adversarial, using hardware memory isolation to confine each device to explicitly granted memory and isolating drivers so that a compromised driver does not compromise the host.
+• In sensitive environments, each host attests to and enforces a closed and immutable policy over everything it will execute. Running anything outside that policy requires the host to zeroize, reboot, and re-attest.
+• Every frontier-tier machine's cryptographic identity is traceable to its point of origin throughout the hardware supply chain.
+• Frontier model weights are encrypted and decryption keys are released only to attested, frontier-tier machines.
+• Every hardware and firmware trust boundary that protects frontier model weights is covered by an automated test suite that asserts its security properties
