-A roadmap for policymakers update
-We recently completed our “roadmap for policymakers” goal and expect to publish a new goal in the coming weeks.
+Policy Target: December 15, 2026
+Policy proposal on embedded third-party evaluators
+Independent evaluation is a core element of our Responsible Scaling Policy and our policy framework for mitigating catastrophic AI risks. We recently committed to going further by embedding independent evaluators, giving them deeper access to models and internal information than external evaluators typically have, while preserving their independence.
+We will develop and publish a policy proposal on how governments can support deeper access for independent evaluators, including how evaluators are funded, what access they need, and how their independence is protected. We will draw on our own experience working with them.
+Policy Target: January 15, 2027
+Policy framework for targeting the pace of AI development
+Our current policy framework focuses on blocking or deterring the deployment of dangerous AI systems. However, we believe policymakers may also need to target the pace of AI development directly, because the automation of AI R&D could accelerate progress faster than governance or safety measures can keep up. Speed itself could become an important risk factor.
+We are working on additional policy proposals that provide options for policymakers to address the risks raised by the speed of AI development, particularly under conditions of recursive self-improvement. We will either publish a proposal or publish an explanation of why we decided not to.
